## 4. Data models — canonical definitions

All documents store `_id` as ObjectId and expose it as `id: str` in API responses.
All timestamps are timezone-aware UTC (`datetime.now(timezone.utc)`), stored as BSON
dates, serialized as ISO 8601 with `Z`.

### 4.1 Central library

**`categories`**

| Field | Type | Required |
|---|---|---|
| `name` | str (1–120) | yes |
| `created_at` | datetime | yes, server-set |
| `updated_at` | datetime | yes, server-set |
| `thumbnail_url` | str \| null | no |
| `image_url` | str \| null | no |

**`subcategories`**

| Field | Type | Required |
|---|---|---|
| `name` | str (1–120) | yes |
| `category_id` | ObjectId → categories | yes |
| `created_at` / `updated_at` | datetime | yes |
| `thumbnail_url`, `image_url` | str \| null | no |

**`assets`**

| Field | Type | Required |
|---|---|---|
| `name` | str (1–200) | yes |
| `description` | str | no, default `""` |
| `category_id` | ObjectId | yes |
| `sub_category_id` | ObjectId | yes |
| `thumbnail_url` | str \| null | no |
| `created_at` / `updated_at` | datetime | yes |
| `more_fields` | object (open schema) | yes, default `{}` |

`more_fields` is the extension point. It is a free-form object but every value must
be a **typed block** (see rule 5). Never store bare scalars at the top level of
`more_fields` other than primitives explicitly allowed by a block definition.

### 4.2 App instances

**`app_instances`**

| Field | Type | Required |
|---|---|---|
| `name` | str | yes, unique |
| `package_name` | str \| null | no, unique when present |
| `app_icon` | str \| null | no |
| `created_at` / `updated_at` | datetime | yes |

**Per-instance content collections** — `instance_categories`,
`instance_subcategories`, `instance_assets`.

**These are reference rows, not duplicated content.** An instance row does not
originate a category, subcategory or asset. It points at a central document and
carries the app's own settings for it, plus any local overrides.

| Field | Type | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `app_instance_id` | ObjectId | Owning app. Always present, always the first index key |
| `source_id` | ObjectId | The central document being referenced. Immutable |
| `is_enabled` | bool | Default `true` |
| `sequence` | int | Default appended to end; gaps allowed |
| `overrides` | object | Sparse. Only fields the app has locally changed |
| `deleted_at` | datetime \| null | Set when removed from this app only |
| `created_at` / `updated_at` | datetime | Of the reference row, not the source |

Instance assets additionally carry `is_premium` (default `false`), `views` (0) and
`downloads` (0).

### Two hard rules — these are load-bearing

**R1. An app instance can never create content.** There is no
`POST /api/v1/app-instances/{id}/categories`, no create form in the instance UI, no
service method that inserts into `categories`, `subcategories` or `assets` from an
instance context. The only way content enters an instance is by referencing something
that already exists centrally. If an operator needs new content for one app, they
create it in the central library first, then reference it. Reject any instance-scoped
create with `409 INSTANCE_CANNOT_CREATE_CONTENT` and a message pointing at the
central endpoint.

**R2. Instance writes never reach central.** Editing an instance item writes to that
row's `overrides` object. Deleting an instance item soft-deletes that row. Neither
path may issue a write against `categories`, `subcategories` or `assets` — not an
update, not a `$inc`, not a `deleted_at`. Services under `services/instance_*.py` and
repositories reached from an instance context must be physically unable to touch the
central collections; enforce this with a repository-level guard, not a code review
habit.

Direction of flow, in one line: **central → instance only, always.**

### Overrides

`overrides` holds only the keys the app changed. Anything absent falls through to the
live central document at read time.

```json
{
  "app_instance_id": "...",
  "source_id": "...",
  "is_enabled": true,
  "sequence": 30,
  "is_premium": true,
  "views": 812,
  "downloads": 140,
  "overrides": {
    "name": "Birthday Frame",
    "more_fields": { "loop_audio": { "type": "audio", "url": "..." } }
  }
}
```

Overridable keys, per type:

| Type | May override |
|---|---|
| category | `name`, `thumbnail_url`, `image_url` |
| subcategory | `name`, `thumbnail_url`, `image_url` |
| asset | `name`, `description`, `thumbnail_url`, `more_fields` |

Never overridable: `category_id`, `sub_category_id`, `source_id`, any timestamp.
An app cannot reparent a referenced asset — that would fork the hierarchy.

`more_fields` overrides are **whole-object replacement**, not a deep merge. A partial
merge across typed blocks is ambiguous and produces content nobody can reason about.
Store the full replacement object; the admin editor pre-fills it from central.

### Resolution at read time

Every instance read resolves in one step:

```
effective = { **central_document, **row.overrides }
        + row.is_enabled, row.sequence, row.is_premium, row.views, row.downloads
        + row.source_id as "sourceId"
```

Do this with a `$lookup` on `source_id` plus `$mergeObjects` in the aggregation
pipeline — one round trip, not N+1 fetches. `services/instance_read_service.py` owns
this and is the only place the merge is implemented.

Consequences to design around, and to state plainly in the admin UI:

- A central edit is visible immediately in every app that references it, **except**
  for fields that app has overridden.
- A central soft-delete makes the item unresolvable. Instance reads must filter out
  rows whose source is deleted, and the admin panel must show them as
  "Source removed" with a single action: remove the reference.
- Clearing an override is a supported action ("Reset to central value") and simply
  `$unset`s the key.

Composite uniqueness: `(app_instance_id, source_id)` is unique per collection — the
same central item cannot be referenced twice by one app.

Referential integrity: an instance asset row may only exist if the app also
references its subcategory and category. Adding an asset reference auto-adds the
missing parent references. Removing a parent reference requires removing its
children, with the count shown first.

