## 10. MongoDB rules

- Collection names: plural snake_case. Field names: snake_case in the database.
  The API may expose camelCase aliases (`categoryId`, `subCategoryId`, `moreFields`)
  via Pydantic `alias` + `populate_by_name=True` — the wire format is camelCase for
  the documented fields, the storage format is snake_case. Be consistent.
- All indexes are declared in `db/indexes.py` and created at startup with
  `create_indexes()`. Never create an index ad hoc in a service.
  Required at minimum:
  - `categories`: `name` (unique, collation strength 2)
  - `subcategories`: `(category_id, name)` unique; `category_id`
  - `assets`: `(category_id, sub_category_id)`; `name` text; `created_at`
  - `app_instances`: `name` unique; `package_name` unique sparse
  - `instance_*`: `(app_instance_id, sequence)`; `(app_instance_id, source_id)` unique;
    `(app_instance_id, is_enabled)`; `source_id` (for reverse "which apps use this?"
    lookups and for orphan detection after a central delete)
  - `instance_assets`: `(app_instance_id, category_id, sub_category_id, sequence)`;
    `(app_instance_id, is_premium)`
  - `api_keys`: `key_prefix` unique; `is_active`
  - `analytics_events`: `(path_template, ts)`; `(app_instance_id, ts)`; TTL on `ts`
  - `monitored_endpoints`: `(method, path_template)` unique
- Deletes are **soft** for categories, subcategories and assets (`deleted_at`).
  Every query must filter `deleted_at: None`. Put that filter in `base.py` so it is
  applied by default and must be explicitly opted out of.
- Deleting a central category requires its subcategories and assets to be empty or
  an explicit `cascade=true`. Report the count before cascading.
- Use `bulk_write` with `ordered=False` for bulk operations. Cap batch size at 500
  items per request.
- Use transactions (multi-document) only for cloning and cascade deletes, and only
  when the deployment is a replica set. Guard with a capability check and a
  documented non-transactional fallback.
- Never use `$where`, `mapReduce`, or unindexed `$regex` prefixed with `.*`.
- **Central write guard.** `repositories/base.py` exposes `CentralRepository` and
  `InstanceRepository`. `InstanceRepository` binds to the `instance_*` collections
  only and raises at construction if handed a central collection. Writes to
  `categories`, `subcategories` or `assets` are reachable only through
  `CentralRepository`, which is never imported by anything under `services/instance_*`
  or `api/v1/instance_content.py`. Add an import-linting test asserting this.
- Deleting a central document must report how many instance references point at it
  (`countDocuments({source_id})` across the three instance collections) before it
  proceeds, and must never cascade the delete into those instance rows — the rows are
  marked unresolvable and surfaced to operators instead.

